<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chandrayaan-3</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chandrayaan-3 was a lunar mission of the Indian Space Research Organisation launched in July 2023. On 23 August 2023 its lander Vikram touched down near the Moon's south pole, the first landing in that region, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the fourth country to achieve a soft landing on the Moon. Its rover Pragyan then studied the lunar surface for about two weeks.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Chandrayaan-3 is the third mission of the Chandrayaan programme, a series of lunar-exploration missions developed by ISRO. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> chandra, Moon, and yana, craft. ISRO had earlier launched Chandrayaan-2, with an orbiter, a lander and a rover. Its lander lost contact with mission control, deviated from its intended trajectory while attempting to land near the lunar south pole, and crashed. Chandrayaan-3 consisted of a lander, Vikram, and a rover, Pragyan, as replacements for those of Chandrayaan-2, together with a propulsion module. The mission aimed to land safely and softly on the Moon, to show the rover driving there, and to carry out experiments on the lunar surface materials.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Chandrayaan-3 was launched on 14 July 2023 aboard an LVM3 M4 rocket from the Satish Dhawan Space Centre in Sriharikota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. It entered lunar orbit on 5 August, and on 17 August the Vikram lander separated from the propulsion module, which had carried it to a lunar orbit of 100 kilometres.</p><p>On 23 August 2023 the lander fired its four engines as a braking manoeuvre at 30 kilometres above the Moon. It then rotated from a horizontal to a vertical position using eight smaller thrusters. It slowed to roughly 150 metres, where it hovered for about 30 seconds to find a good landing spot, and touched down near the lunar south pole, at 69 degrees south, at 12:33 UTC.</p><p>The rover's laser-induced breakdown spectroscope reported sulfur near the south pole, and ISRO released temperature profiles from the top 10 centimetres of the lunar surface, in which a high range of 70 °C near the surface was not expected. The lander also made a short hop of about 40 centimetres. The lander and rover were built to operate for one lunar daylight period, or 14 Earth days, and not to endure the night temperatures of minus 120 °C. The rover was put into sleep mode on 3 September, and neither responded to wake-up calls later in September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With the landing, ISRO became the fourth national space agency to land successfully on the Moon, after the Soviet space programme, NASA and CNSA, and the first to make a soft landing near the lunar south pole. The Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narendra-modi">Narendra Modi</a>, named the touchdown point Station Shiv Shakti and declared 23 August National Space Day. The live stream of the landing on ISRO's YouTube channel had eight million concurrent viewers, the highest on YouTube for a live video. ISRO chairman S. Somanath said that the agency had learnt a lot from its failure and corrected it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chandrayaan-3” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
